              "text": "The accepted, \"official\" pronunciation of **doge** (as in the meme, the cryptocurrency Dogecoin, and the government agency acronym DOGE) is **\u201cdohj\u201d**. \n\n---\n\n### How to Say It\n* **Phonetic Spelling:** `/do\u028ad\u0292/` or `/do\u028a\u0292/`\n* **How it sounds:** \n  * It starts with a long **\"Doh\"** (like \"dough\" or \"go\").\n  * It ends with a soft **\"j\"** or **\"zh\"** sound (like the \"g\" in *gell* or the \"s\" in *measure* and *treasure*).\n  * **The Rhyme:** It rhymes with **\"vogue,\"** but with a soft \"j\" sound at the end instead of a hard \"g.\" \n\n---\n\n### Where Did This Pronunciation Come From?\nThe pronunciation actually predates the meme. It comes from a June 24, 2005 episode of the puppet/web-series ***Homestar Runner*** (specifically the episode \"Biz Cas Fri 1\"). \n\nIn the video, the character Homestar Runner tries to distract Strong Bad by calling him his \"D-O-G-E\". Strong Bad replies: *\"My d-o-g-e? What is that, a **dohj**?\"*\n\nWhen the Shiba Inu meme took off in 2013 and Dogecoin was created, the founders of the cryptocurrency explicitly cited this *Homestar Runner* clip as the official way to pronounce the name.\n\n---\n\n### Common (But \"Incorrect\") Alternatives\nBecause \"doge\" is visually just the word \"dog\" with an \"e\" tacked onto the end, people have come up with several of their own pronunciations over the years:\n\n1. **\"Dohg\"** \u2014 Rhymes exactly with \"vogue\" or \"rogue,\" ending in a hard *g*.\n2. **\"Dodge\"** \u2014 Pronounced exactly like the car brand or the game \"dodgeball\".\n3. **\"Dog-gy\"** \u2014 Pronounced like \"doggy,\" since the meme is literally a picture of a dog.\n\n*(While many people still use these, if you say \"dohj\" to a crypto enthusiast or a chronic internet user, they will know exactly what you mean).*\n\n---\n\n### Fun Fact: The Historical \"Doge\"\nThe word \"doge\" is also a real historical English word. It refers to the elected chief magistrate/ruler of the former republics of Venice and Genoa during the medieval and Renaissance periods. \n\nHistorically, English speakers also pronounce this word **\"dohj\"** (rhymes with \"vogue\" but with a soft \"j\"). In standard Italian, however, it is pronounced as two syllables: **\"doh-jay\"**.",
